            "My mother kept two clocks in the kitchen. One showed the time in the city where we lived, the other the time in the town she had left thirty years earlier. As a child, I assumed this was a practical arrangement, a way of knowing when it was sensible to telephone her sisters. Only much later did I understand that the second clock was not really for telephoning. It was a way of living in two places at once.",
            "We moved when I was four, too young to remember the journey but old enough, apparently, to cry for weeks for a grandmother I would not see again for six years. My parents came for work, as millions of people have, and they stayed, as many more do not plan to. Admittedly, their story was a fortunate one. They were not fleeing war or poverty; they had jobs waiting, and the right papers. Yet even a fortunate migration, I have come to think, involves a kind of loss that is rarely spoken about, because it seems ungrateful to mention it.",
            "That loss is difficult to describe because it is not a single thing. It is the loss of being understood without explanation: of jokes that need no translation, of a shared sense of what is polite, of the names of plants and streets and childhood games. My parents learned the new language well, but they never dreamed in it. Tellingly, when they were angry or very tired, they returned, without noticing, to the language of the town behind the second clock.",
            "For children like me, the situation was reversed. I grew up speaking the new language at school and the old one at home, and gradually the balance shifted until the language of home became the one I spoke with an accent. On summer visits to my grandmother's town, my cousins teased me gently for words I used wrongly. In the city where we lived, classmates sometimes asked where I was 'really' from. Oddly enough, I felt most foreign in the two places that should have been home.",
            "Researchers who study migration have a vocabulary for this experience. They speak of assimilation, integration and hybrid identities, of diasporas that maintain connections across borders, of a 'third culture' that belongs fully to neither the old country nor the new. These terms are useful, but they can make the experience sound tidier than it is. A hybrid identity is not a comfortable mixture, like a well-made dish. It is often closer to a conversation that never quite ends, between parts of yourself that do not always agree.",
            "And yet, the longer I live with it, the less I see this as a problem to be solved. The kitchen with two clocks, I now realise, was not a place of division but of translation. It was where my mother cooked the dishes of her childhood with ingredients from the local market, adapting each recipe a little every year. It was where neighbours from four or five countries came for coffee and argued, in a mixture of languages, about everything from politics to the right way to make rice. It was, in a sense, a small country of its own.",
            "Today, a remarkable share of the world's population lives in a country other than the one in which they were born, and many more live far from the region where they grew up. In the cities of the Gulf, where a large part of the population comes from elsewhere, this experience is not unusual at all; it is normal. That makes the question of belonging one of the defining questions of our time. Does a person belong where they were born, where their family comes from, where they live and pay taxes, or where they feel at ease?",
            "I have no neat answer, and I distrust those who do. What I have learned is that belonging is not only something you find; it is also something you make, through habits and relationships, through the slow work of becoming known to the people around you. My mother never stopped missing the town behind the second clock. But when she finally visited it after retiring, she came back after a month and said, a little surprised at herself, that she had missed her kitchen.",
