            "The difficulty is that climate change is a problem of collective action on a global scale. The benefits of burning fossil fuels go mainly to those who burn them, while the costs are shared by everyone, including people who have contributed very little and generations not yet born. No single country can solve the problem alone, yet every country is tempted to let others make the sacrifices. Agreeing on a fair division of the burden is therefore not a side issue. It is the central political challenge. It also involves two different kinds of action: mitigation, which means cutting emissions to limit future warming, and adaptation, which means protecting people from the warming that can no longer be avoided.",
            "Three principles dominate the debate. The first is historical responsibility. Carbon dioxide remains in the atmosphere for centuries, so the warming we experience today is the result of cumulative emissions over more than a hundred and fifty years. Countries that industrialised early produced a large share of those emissions. On this view, they should carry the largest share of the burden, much as a factory that polluted a river for decades would be expected to pay for cleaning it.",
            "Critics of this principle raise two objections. Many of the people who produced those historical emissions are no longer alive, and they did not know the consequences of their actions. Is it fair to hold their descendants responsible? Defenders reply that the descendants continue to enjoy the wealth those emissions helped to create, so it is reasonable for them to share the cost as well.",
